1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,566
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,586 --> 00:00:07,485
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,885 --> 00:00:15,216
This is Lecture 19, the prototyping platform and the parts library.

4
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:19,201
Over the last few lectures we covered motors, solenoids,

5
00:00:19,221 --> 00:00:21,711
control and the control box one by one.

6
00:00:21,731 --> 00:00:26,603
Today we put them together into a prototype that really sews. Which modules,

7
00:00:26,623 --> 00:00:30,723
how to size them, how to write the firmware, and how to test it.

8
00:00:31,123 --> 00:00:37,638
How to write requirements and library entries, the AI selection assistant,

9
00:00:37,658 --> 00:00:44,172
the state machine and the five gates from prototype to product are left to

10
00:00:44,192 --> 00:00:45,143
the slides.

11
00:00:46,663 --> 00:00:47,963
Here's the problem.

12
00:00:48,363 --> 00:00:54,149
A team wants to turn a lockstitch head, its clutch motor removed,

13
00:00:54,169 --> 00:00:59,955
into an electronically controlled prototype: pedal speed control,

14
00:00:59,975 --> 00:01:04,957
5000 r/min top speed, 0 to 5000 in at most 0.15 seconds,

15
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:10,227
load inertia 5 times 10 to the minus 4; 30 starts a minute,

16
00:01:10,247 --> 00:01:16,301
sewing half the time; automatic trimming when the pedal is released,

17
00:01:16,321 --> 00:01:22,196
with a trimming solenoid needing 3 millimetres and 12 newtons and,

18
00:01:22,216 --> 00:01:28,181
at 300 r/min, pull-in and release within 20 degrees of shaft angle.

19
00:01:28,581 --> 00:01:39,773
The library offers 250, 400, 550 and 750-watt motors and 1.5,

20
00:01:39,793 --> 00:01:43,265
3 and 5-amp drives.

21
00:01:43,665 --> 00:01:45,044
How do we choose?

22
00:01:46,564 --> 00:01:51,918
The four machines look very different, but their control skeleton is the same:

23
00:01:51,938 --> 00:01:53,640
one master sets the time,

24
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,567
several actuators act according to the master's position,

25
00:01:57,587 --> 00:01:59,083
sensors report status,

26
00:01:59,103 --> 00:02:04,733
the controller schedules everything in between and sends data to the plant system.

27
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:11,593
WQ-SC fixes that skeleton as five layers plus mechanics.

28
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:15,517
At the bottom, actuators and sensors:

29
00:02:15,537 --> 00:02:22,797
the spindle is a permanent-magnet synchronous servo whose 2500-line encoder,

30
00:02:22,817 --> 00:02:27,874
multiplied by four, gives ten thousand counts a turn;

31
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:31,515
feed and X–Y use closed-loop steppers;

32
00:02:31,535 --> 00:02:35,730
trimming and wiping use push-pull solenoids.

33
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:41,553
Above that come the drive layer, the real-time control layer,

34
00:02:41,573 --> 00:02:47,978
the HMI and network layer, and at the top the digital-factory interface.

35
00:02:49,498 --> 00:02:52,958
Why make shaft angle the time base?

36
00:02:53,358 --> 00:03:00,200
Because shaft speed isn't constant: slow at the start, fast in the middle,

37
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:02,704
slow again before trimming.

38
00:03:03,104 --> 00:03:10,032
Trimming, backtacking and X–Y moves must line up with mechanism positions,

39
00:03:10,052 --> 00:03:17,450
such as whether the needle is in the fabric or the feed dog is below the plate.

40
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:24,284
So the firmware says energise when the shaft reaches 205 degrees,

41
00:03:24,304 --> 00:03:30,142
not 8 milliseconds after top dead centre; written by angle,

42
00:03:30,162 --> 00:03:32,625
it lines up at any speed.

43
00:03:33,025 --> 00:03:38,761
Actuators have delays, so convert the delay into an angle,

44
00:03:38,781 --> 00:03:46,501
6 times n times t degrees, and check it at the speed where the action happens.

45
00:03:46,901 --> 00:03:52,021
The flat knitter has no shaft, so its master is the carriage position.

46
00:03:53,541 --> 00:04:00,161
Watch the parts-library configurator set up the four machines in turn.

47
00:04:00,561 --> 00:04:03,254
Controller, gateway, solenoid drive,

48
00:04:03,274 --> 00:04:07,248
power supply and firmware framework are fully shared;

49
00:04:07,268 --> 00:04:12,448
the differences are concentrated in three places: what the master is,

50
00:04:12,468 --> 00:04:18,025
how many position axes there are, and which actions the solenoids perform.

51
00:04:18,425 --> 00:04:20,910
After the lockstitch machine,

52
00:04:20,930 --> 00:04:28,166
the overlock is just a new requirements table with fewer solenoids and an air valve;

53
00:04:28,186 --> 00:04:31,621
the pattern sewer adds two stepper axes;

54
00:04:31,641 --> 00:04:36,285
the knitter swaps the spindle master for the carriage.

55
00:04:36,685 --> 00:04:40,691
Blue part numbers are existing library entries;

56
00:04:40,711 --> 00:04:44,545
yellow rows differ from the default solution.

57
00:04:46,065 --> 00:04:53,156
Rapid configuration: compute criteria, take the cheapest that passes.

58
00:04:54,676 --> 00:04:55,656
Now the spindle.

59
00:04:56,056 --> 00:05:04,215
Accelerating from rest to top speed takes total inertia times angular acceleration,

60
00:05:04,235 --> 00:05:08,551
plus 0.15 newton-metres of Coulomb friction.

61
00:05:08,951 --> 00:05:16,160
For the 400-watt motor the total inertia is 6 times 10 to the minus 4,

62
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:24,113
the angular acceleration about 3491, and the torque about 2.24 newton-metres,

63
00:05:24,133 --> 00:05:27,521
within 0.8 of the 3.2 peak, 2.56.

64
00:05:27,921 --> 00:05:35,354
The RMS torque over the duty cycle is 0.89, within the 1.27 rating,

65
00:05:35,374 --> 00:05:39,581
and the inertia ratio is 5, within 10.

66
00:05:39,981 --> 00:05:47,397
The 250-watt motor falls short on both acceleration and RMS torque;

67
00:05:47,417 --> 00:05:51,281
the 550 passes too, but costs more.

68
00:05:52,801 --> 00:05:59,249
This is the criteria column of the configurator: spindle, servo drive,

69
00:05:59,269 --> 00:06:06,549
solenoids and solenoid driver, each showing what's needed and what's available.

70
00:06:06,949 --> 00:06:10,089
Auto-configure first and everything is ticked.

71
00:06:10,489 --> 00:06:19,823
Swap the motor to 250 watts and the acceleration torque and RMS torque checks fail.

72
00:06:20,223 --> 00:06:25,275
Put the 400 back and swap the drive to 1.5 amps,

73
00:06:25,295 --> 00:06:31,299
and the continuous current and E-stop energy checks fail.

74
00:06:31,698 --> 00:06:34,239
Every verdict can point to a number,

75
00:06:34,258 --> 00:06:39,999
and that's the difference between rapid configuration and picking by gut feeling.

76
00:06:41,519 --> 00:06:45,721
Each solenoid action gets three checks: enough stroke,

77
00:06:45,741 --> 00:06:51,508
enough force at the end of stroke, and, for actions while the shaft turns,

78
00:06:51,528 --> 00:06:53,619
the angle it turns through.

79
00:06:54,019 --> 00:06:57,892
Trimming happens at 300 r/min;

80
00:06:57,912 --> 00:07:05,288
the M15 pulls in in 6.9 milliseconds and releases in 5.1,

81
00:07:05,308 --> 00:07:11,257
which is 12.4 and 9.2 degrees, both within 20,

82
00:07:11,277 --> 00:07:15,799
and it works up to about 483 r/min.

83
00:07:16,199 --> 00:07:26,937
Backtack switching happens at 1800 r/min; the L30 takes 118.8 degrees,

84
00:07:26,957 --> 00:07:33,391
which fits in the 201.6-degree idle angle.

85
00:07:33,791 --> 00:07:42,216
The power supply: logic plus hold plus half the boost, times 1.2,

86
00:07:42,236 --> 00:07:47,153
about 132 watts, so a 150-watt supply.

87
00:07:48,673 --> 00:07:50,333
Firmware and testing.

88
00:07:51,853 --> 00:07:53,313
Now the firmware.

89
00:07:53,713 --> 00:07:59,681
The process layer revolves around three data-driven things: counts to angle,

90
00:07:59,701 --> 00:08:05,827
an event table looked up by angle, and a state machine that governs the cycle.

91
00:08:06,227 --> 00:08:11,136
The encoder gives ten thousand counts a turn;

92
00:08:11,156 --> 00:08:17,708
the firmware's angle unit is 0.1 degree, 0 to 3599 per turn,

93
00:08:17,728 --> 00:08:24,061
and the conversion is counts times 9 over 25, in integers.

94
00:08:24,461 --> 00:08:32,756
At 300 r/min 0.1 degree lasts about 56 microseconds, at 5000 only about 3.3,

95
00:08:32,776 --> 00:08:34,944
so polling won't do:

96
00:08:34,964 --> 00:08:43,149
compute the count for the next event angle in advance and write it into the

97
00:08:43,169 --> 00:08:46,759
encoder timer's compare register.

98
00:08:47,159 --> 00:08:55,259
One action per row of the event table; a new head means new data, not new code.

99
00:08:56,779 --> 00:09:02,199
Most of the firmware logic can be tested before the prototype is even assembled.

100
00:09:02,599 --> 00:09:06,890
There are three levels running the same test cases,

101
00:09:06,910 --> 00:09:11,876
with only the unit under test and the plant model changing.

102
00:09:12,276 --> 00:09:16,931
Level one, software in the loop: compile the process layer,

103
00:09:16,951 --> 00:09:20,022
state machine and event table for a PC,

104
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:26,123
replace the hardware abstraction layer with simulated drivers and connect the

105
00:09:26,143 --> 00:09:27,550
book's lab models,

106
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:31,036
50 microseconds a step, run on every commit.

107
00:09:31,436 --> 00:09:34,079
Level two, hardware in the loop:

108
00:09:34,099 --> 00:09:40,737
the real controller and firmware against a plant model on a real-time simulator,

109
00:09:40,757 --> 00:09:47,062
which at 5000 r/min must interpolate about 42 encoder counts evenly every 50

110
00:09:47,082 --> 00:09:48,143
microseconds;

111
00:09:48,163 --> 00:09:51,056
its biggest use is fault injection.

112
00:09:51,456 --> 00:09:53,556
Level three is the test bench.

113
00:09:55,076 --> 00:10:04,176
Building a gantry glue-dispensing robot follows the same process as WQ-SC.

114
00:10:04,576 --> 00:10:11,197
Write a numeric requirements table: stroke, speed, acceleration, payload,

115
00:10:11,217 --> 00:10:12,471
repeatability.

116
00:10:12,871 --> 00:10:19,854
Size a servo or closed-loop stepper for each axis with T equals J alpha plus

117
00:10:19,874 --> 00:10:24,277
T_f and the inertia-ratio and RMS-torque checks,

118
00:10:24,297 --> 00:10:30,544
pick the cheapest size in the library that passes, and generate BOM,

119
00:10:30,564 --> 00:10:35,888
I/O table and controller configuration from the same data.

120
00:10:36,288 --> 00:10:39,477
The glue valve isn't timed either:

121
00:10:39,497 --> 00:10:45,517
it's written as open while X is between 120 and 180 millimetres,

122
00:10:45,537 --> 00:10:49,953
the position cam switch of a motion controller,

123
00:10:49,973 --> 00:10:55,333
a one-to-one match with this lecture's angle event table.

124
00:10:56,853 --> 00:11:02,075
Building your own PC is rapid configuration too.

125
00:11:02,475 --> 00:11:08,255
Decide the needs first: which games, which software.

126
00:11:08,655 --> 00:11:16,948
Then choose the motherboard, CPU and graphics card by interface and power.

127
00:11:17,348 --> 00:11:23,808
Finally add up every part's power, add a margin, and pick the power supply.

128
00:11:24,208 --> 00:11:30,633
That's exactly how this lecture sized the 24-volt supply:

129
00:11:30,653 --> 00:11:35,948
logic plus hold plus half the boost, times 1.2.

130
00:11:36,348 --> 00:11:42,756
And PC builders make the same mistakes: they look only at peak performance,

131
00:11:42,776 --> 00:11:45,842
forget the power supply and cooling,

132
00:11:45,862 --> 00:11:49,528
and the machine reboots under a heavy load.

133
00:11:51,048 --> 00:11:52,860
Back to the opening question.

134
00:11:53,260 --> 00:12:04,067
Choose the 400-watt motor with the 3-amp drive: acceleration torque 2.24,

135
00:12:04,087 --> 00:12:12,818
within 2.56; RMS torque 0.89, within 1.27; inertia ratio 5.

136
00:12:13,218 --> 00:12:22,037
The 250 fails both torque checks; the 550 passes but costs more.

137
00:12:22,437 --> 00:12:30,691
The cheaper 1.5-amp drive exceeds its continuous rating at 1.79 amps and

138
00:12:30,711 --> 00:12:35,518
can't absorb the 57.6-joule E-stop energy.

139
00:12:35,918 --> 00:12:44,297
The M15 takes 12.4 and 9.2 degrees at 300 r/min, within 20.

140
00:12:44,697 --> 00:12:51,369
The whole BOM comes to about 2455 yuan.

141
00:12:52,889 --> 00:12:56,582
Now Virtual lab 19-1, the parts-library configurator,

142
00:12:56,602 --> 00:13:00,225
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The criteria,

143
00:13:00,245 --> 00:13:03,097
tasks and total on the right are blurred;

144
00:13:03,117 --> 00:13:08,351
they're for you to work out in the lab. Top left is the requirements table,

145
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:12,974
in the middle the BOM. It opens with a previous prototype's parts;

146
00:13:12,994 --> 00:13:16,197
before configuring, check which criteria fail.

147
00:13:16,597 --> 00:13:19,130
Click Configure automatically.

148
00:13:19,150 --> 00:13:25,683
For each category the configurator checks every size against the criteria and

149
00:13:25,703 --> 00:13:28,322
takes the cheapest that passes.

150
00:13:28,342 --> 00:13:32,577
Check it against the worked example in the slides.

151
00:13:32,977 --> 00:13:36,316
Task one: in the BOM drop-down,

152
00:13:36,336 --> 00:13:44,550
swap the servo drive to the 1.5-amp size by hand see which criteria fail and

153
00:13:44,570 --> 00:13:45,850
by how much,

154
00:13:45,870 --> 00:13:51,268
and compare with the worked example in the slides.

155
00:13:51,668 --> 00:13:53,642
Put the 3-amp drive back,

156
00:13:53,662 --> 00:14:00,105
then raise the trimming speed from 300 and watch the trim solenoid's angle check.

157
00:14:00,125 --> 00:14:02,818
See where it goes past 20 degrees,

158
00:14:02,838 --> 00:14:08,004
and compare with the top trimming speed worked out in the slides.

159
00:14:08,404 --> 00:14:14,100
Task two: switch to the overlock, reset the requirements, auto-configure,

160
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:16,450
and note the design and total.

161
00:14:16,470 --> 00:14:22,323
Then change only the top speed to 7000 without swapping parts and see which

162
00:14:22,343 --> 00:14:23,263
checks fail.

163
00:14:23,283 --> 00:14:28,118
Which modules a new configuration replaces, and what it costs,

164
00:14:28,138 --> 00:14:29,449
is yours to find.

165
00:14:29,849 --> 00:14:35,989
Task three: switch to the pattern sewer, auto-configure,

166
00:14:36,009 --> 00:14:42,149
and note each axis's drive, motor and natural frequency.

167
00:14:42,549 --> 00:14:47,859
Then change the Y drive to a ball screw, see which checks fail,

168
00:14:47,879 --> 00:14:52,259
and think about how the lead multiplies motor speed.

169
00:14:52,279 --> 00:14:56,489
The guide and report template are on the platform.

170
00:14:58,009 --> 00:15:03,965
Everything in this lecture is on the WenQuest learning platform.

171
00:15:04,365 --> 00:15:10,308
You can open today's interactive slides and the parts-library configurator

172
00:15:10,328 --> 00:15:12,161
and work them yourself;

173
00:15:12,181 --> 00:15:15,384
the requirements table, library entries,

174
00:15:15,404 --> 00:15:21,105
state machine and five gates that this video skipped are in the slides.

175
00:15:21,505 --> 00:15:25,762
You submit the assignment and the lab report on the platform,

176
00:15:25,782 --> 00:15:28,005
and get marks and comments back.

177
00:15:28,405 --> 00:15:32,215
Questions go to the course discussion forum,

178
00:15:32,235 --> 00:15:37,874
and the grades page shows your mark on every assignment and quiz.

179
00:15:39,394 --> 00:15:45,281
Your assignment has four questions about one heavy-fabric lockstitch head. One:

180
00:15:45,301 --> 00:15:48,945
with a load inertia of 6 times 10 to the minus 4,

181
00:15:48,965 --> 00:15:52,684
a top speed of 4500 r/min reached in 0.12 seconds,

182
00:15:52,704 --> 00:15:58,441
run every motor size through the seven criteria and choose the cheapest motor

183
00:15:58,461 --> 00:15:59,563
and drive pair.

184
00:15:59,963 --> 00:16:04,102
Two: heavy-fabric trimming happens at 400 r/min.

185
00:16:04,122 --> 00:16:08,000
Which of M15 and L30 stays within 20 degrees,

186
00:16:08,020 --> 00:16:14,585
and which requirement fields would you change to check it in the lab? Three:

187
00:16:14,605 --> 00:16:20,909
with a directly driven knife the trim starts between 290 and 300 degrees.

188
00:16:20,929 --> 00:16:24,202
Write the trim row of the event table,

189
00:16:24,222 --> 00:16:30,873
and explain why it must not be written as energise 180 milliseconds after the

190
00:16:30,893 --> 00:16:32,432
pedal is released.

191
00:16:32,832 --> 00:16:37,564
Four is a discussion: the prototype trims at 99.7 percent,

192
00:16:37,584 --> 00:16:42,152
a teammate changes the wiper angle, success drops to 98,

193
00:16:42,172 --> 00:16:48,296
and nobody can say what changed. Using this lecture's approach to versions,

194
00:16:48,316 --> 00:16:52,966
tests and reporting, design a way to trace such problems.

195
00:16:53,366 --> 00:16:56,533
Submit the report on the WenQuest platform.

196
00:16:58,053 --> 00:17:02,165
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

197
00:17:02,185 --> 00:17:08,902
a spindle with a total inertia of 7 times 10 to the minus 4 must reach 4500

198
00:17:08,922 --> 00:17:11,776
r/min from rest in 0.15 seconds,

199
00:17:11,796 --> 00:17:18,513
with 0.15 newton-metres of friction. What acceleration torque does it need?

200
00:17:18,913 --> 00:17:27,019
Or, at 5000 r/min the spindle turns 0.1 degree in just over three microseconds;

201
00:17:27,039 --> 00:17:34,733
can event scheduling poll the angle in a task that runs once a millisecond?

202
00:17:35,133 --> 00:17:40,833
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

203
00:17:42,353 --> 00:17:44,448
Three things to remember.

204
00:17:44,848 --> 00:17:51,249
One: WQ-SC uses a five-layer reference architecture plus mechanics,

205
00:17:51,269 --> 00:17:55,562
building four prototypes from one controller,

206
00:17:55,582 --> 00:18:02,463
module set and firmware framework, differing in master and actuator mix.

207
00:18:02,863 --> 00:18:08,265
Two: rapid configuration: numbers in the requirements table,

208
00:18:08,285 --> 00:18:15,042
compute the criteria, take the cheapest size that passes, and generate BOM,

209
00:18:15,062 --> 00:18:19,832
wiring and firmware configuration from the same data,

210
00:18:19,852 --> 00:18:24,170
without forgetting E-stop energy and RMS torque.

211
00:18:24,570 --> 00:18:25,064
Three:

212
00:18:25,084 --> 00:18:31,583
the firmware writes its event table by shaft angle and runs the cycle with a

213
00:18:31,603 --> 00:18:32,784
state machine,

214
00:18:32,804 --> 00:18:37,159
verified step by step through software in the loop,

215
00:18:37,179 --> 00:18:40,590
hardware in the loop and the test bench.

216
00:18:40,990 --> 00:18:46,645
The assignment, quiz and lab report are on WenQuest.

217
00:18:47,045 --> 00:18:49,835
The slides, the virtual lab,

218
00:18:49,855 --> 00:18:56,861
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

219
00:18:56,881 --> 00:19:03,585
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

220
00:19:03,985 --> 00:19:08,857
Next time we carry today's method all the way through:

221
00:19:08,877 --> 00:19:13,841
control prototypes for the lockstitch and the overlock,

222
00:19:13,861 --> 00:19:19,005
from wiring and firmware to the test bench. See you then.
